    "chunk_text": "\u201cThis is the third edition, bigger than before, with more authors and more countries represented,\u201d he said. \u201cThe more voices we bring together, the richer and more interesting our conversations become.\u201d\n\nHe recounted the journey of a young participant who attended the first edition as a student, later entered and won the Future Writers Competition, received mentorship and subsequently authored a book that won a literary prize.\n\n\u201cThat is what truly matters,\u201d Surgenor said. \u201cIt\u2019s not just about what we do today, but about inspiring future generations to contribute and to love literature and the arts as much as we do.\u201d\n\nHSBC also showcased its parallel community initiatives, including spoken English programmes in hill country communities and a Female Entrepreneurs Programme supporting women-led small businesses. Ten entrepreneurs are exhibiting their products at the festival site.\n\nHighlighting environmental concerns, Surgenor drew attention to efforts to clean Beira Lake, where two waste-collection boats remove nearly 200 kilogrammes of debris daily as part of a broader awareness campaign.\n\nThe opening ceremony concluded with a traditional Sri Lankan dance performance by the Saranda Dance Academy, underscoring the festival\u2019s commitment to celebrating local cultural heritage alongside international voices.\n\nOrganisers expect around 500 visitors each day, as the festival continues with panel discussions on history, food, sport, fiction, health and contemporary issues, reaffirming Colombo\u2019s place on the region\u2019s cultural calendar.\n\nSudden summons to meet visiting Pakistan PM & important appointments\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
