    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e3b71badda3165401abbc4c6f171ef8d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1939,
    "chunk_text": "Lankem Ceylon, Axalta Coating Systems strengthen partnership in high-performance industrial coatings\n\nAxalta Coating Systems Business Director \u2013 Refinish and Industrial, South Asia and Value Line ASEAN Lokender Pal Singh (left) with Lankem Ceylon Managing Director Suren Goonewardene at the announcement of the expanded partnership to introduce advanced industrial coating technologies to Sri Lanka\n\nLankem Ceylon PLC, the authorised partner of Axalta Coating Systems in Sri Lanka, has announced the expansion of its collaboration with Axalta to strengthen access to high-performance industrial coating technologies in the country. The development follows the successful introduction of Axalta\u2019s globally recognised automotive coating products to the Sri Lankan market.\n\nUnder the expanded partnership, Lankem will expand the availability of advanced industrial liquid, marine, and powder coating solutions tailored to meet the protection needs of key sectors including infrastructure, energy, agriculture, heavy equipment, rail, and steel structures.\n\nThe initiative aims to address the growing demand for durable coating systems that enhance operational life while reducing maintenance frequency and associated costs.\n\nAxalta Coating Systems brings more than 150 years of coatings expertise and serves over 100,000 customers across 130 countries. Its high-performance coatings are engineered to prevent corrosion, enhance durability, and improve productivity across a wide range of industrial and infrastructure applications.\n\nA key feature of these coating systems is their ability to be applied directly onto clean, firmly adherent rust. Through stabilising corrosion rather than merely covering it, the coatings reduce the need for extensive blast cleaning and surface preparation. This capability minimises downtime and allows critical infrastructure and industrial facilities to remain operational during maintenance activities."
