    "md5": "d43e2b9ea19a3a5c0920c5e60489e3b7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1477,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cJayaflava has been a mad, chaotic, fun and slightly unhinged journey but also a real eye opener and I envisioned my Sri Lankan cookbook to be real and not diluted nor apologetic and represent my home as I knew it- multi-ethnic, modern, generous, opinionated and completely obsessed with flavour. The birth of the TV series stemmed from my realisation that I should focus beyond the recipes, to the streets where koththu was chopped at midnight, the village kitchens, bars and beaches that breathe the soul of Sri Lanka,\u201d Marikkar explained at the media conference held at Cinnamon Life at City of Dreams on Tuesday.\n\nFilmed across the island over a duration of two years, spanning from Colombo to Jaffna, Hiriketiya to Puttalam and skirting Bentota to Galle, Jayaflava celebrates not just bold and flavourful cuisine but also a modern and fresh perspective of island life, each episode focusing on an iconic dish, and following it outward to its origin, a village, a kitchen and a story- koththu in Colombo, crab curry in Jaffna, good times in Hiriketiya, hoppers along the south coast and Colombo, coconut in Puttalam and lamprais in Galle.\n\nAudiences will also meet some of the most influential voices shaping the Sri Lankan food and cultural journey, chef Dharshana Munidasa from Ministry of Crab, Lahiru and Don from Smoke and Bitters, booker prize winning author Shehan Karunathilake, chef Karan Gokani of London Hoppers and broadcaster and author Nihal Aththanayake."
  },
