    "md5": "2761ab516d6e0e03b916d330421ab844",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1818,
    "chunk_text": "Victor, the Friend of the Foreign Press\n\nThis article was written by Amal Jayasinghe, Bureau Chief of Agence France-Presse in Colombo to commemorate Ravaya Founder/Editor who\u2019s first death anniversary fell last month.\n\nTo most Sri Lankans, Victor Ivan was an editor marked by his role in the failed 1971 JVP insurrection. To the foreign press, he was more than a revolutionary turned reporter.What many may not know is his close association with foreign correspondents and visiting journalists during one of the most turbulent periods since the 1971 JVP-led insurrection, in which he himself was involved.\n\nWhen a new association for foreign journalists in Sri Lanka was formed in mid-1988, Victor was one of the first Sri Lankan editors to strongly support it. He later became an Associate Member, a category opened within the Foreign Correspondents\u2019 Association (FCA) to those interested in the work of foreign correspondents. This was a time when international media attention was sharply focused on Sri Lanka after it signed a controversial peace pact with neighbouring India to try to end Tamil separatism, but instead ended up fighting the separatists.\n\nMore than 50 foreign media outlets \u2014 such as ABC, BBC and CBC \u2014 and international wire services including Agence France-Presse, Associated Press, Deutsche Presse-Agentur and Reuters, as well as regional newspapers such as The Hindu, The Times of India and Hindustan Times, were represented in Colombo.\n\nAt the time, the FCA held regular meetings, panel discussions and social events. This was when television talk shows were unheard of and before the World Wide Web was invented. Victor was a regular guest at the FCA and a valuable resource not only for visiting \u201cfiremen\u201d, but also for those based in Colombo, helping them understand the story."
  },
