    "chunk_size_bytes": 2063,
    "chunk_text": "At the time, the FCA held regular meetings, panel discussions and social events. This was when television talk shows were unheard of and before the World Wide Web was invented. Victor was a regular guest at the FCA and a valuable resource not only for visiting \u201cfiremen\u201d, but also for those based in Colombo, helping them understand the story.\n\nI was one of the younger reporters who regularly turned to Victor to pick his brains and understand the background to political developments. He was always upfront about his political bias and never pretended to be an independent journalist. Victor told me many times that he was biased towards what he believed to be right and just. In later life, he would confess that he was not always right.\n\nHe had strongly supported Chandrika Kumaratunga\u2019s rise to power in 1994, but soon grew disillusioned. He questioned her policies and key appointments, notably the appointment of Sarath Nanda Silva as Chief Justice in September 1999. Three days later, Ravaya took the unusual step of publishing Silva\u2019s swearing-in ceremony upside down on its front page. Instead of black letters on a white background, the paper was reversed, with white letters on a black background. \u201c\u0d85\u0db0\u0dd2\u0d9a\u0dbb\u0dab \u0db1\u0dd2\u0daf\u0dc4\u0dc3\u0dda \u0db8\u0dc5\u0d9c\u0db8\u201d (Death of the Independence of the Judiciary) was the headline.\n\nVictor predicted the downfall of Mahinda Rajapaksa when he amended the Constitution and sought a third term in office. \u201cHe is like a bulldozer without brakes going downhill,\u201d Victor said when I sought his analysis of the 2015 presidential election. He understood the value of a good soundbite. Beyond politics and conflict, Victor was also my authority on Sri Lanka\u2019s complex social structures, including the caste system and its significance in elections and governance.\n\nMost foreign correspondents \u2014 myself included \u2014 regarded Victor as a reliable \u201cdial-a-quote\u201d: a trusted, knowledgeable voice who invariably enriched our stories.\n\nThe Tsunami of 2024 and what followed at the Finance Ministry and overseas"
