    "chunk_text": "Hambantota solar projects: Allegations of bribery and environmental destruction \u2013\u2013 Sajeewa Chamikara speaks out\n\nEnvironmental activist Sajeewa Chamikara has alleged that several solar power projects in the Hambantota District have resumed operations following the payment of bribes, resulting in extensive destruction within a designated wildlife management reserve.\n\nChamikara, representing the Movement for Land and Agricultural Reform (MONLAR), said that projects previously halted by the Hambantota District Development Committee have now recommenced after what he described as irregular financial transactions amounting to Rs. 14 million.\n\nAccording to him, five companies had jointly provided the funds to facilitate the construction of an electric fence spanning approximately 12 km from Nagarawawa to Mayurapura and Usagala within the Hambantota Wildlife Management Reserve.\n\nA collapsing notice board with a message urging the public to protect the environment\n\nHe alleged that around Rs. 11 million had been channelled for the fencing work, while the remaining Rs. 3 million had been distributed in a manner that has not been properly disclosed.\n\nHe alleged that after providing the funds, these companies resumed clearing forest lands within the wildlife reserve to construct solar power facilities.\n\n\u201cNearly 1,000 acres of forest land within the elephant management reserve have been cleared using heavy machinery and in some instances by setting fires,\u201d Chamikara charged. \u201cThis has caused severe and irreversible environmental damage in areas where elephants and other wildlife live.\u201d\n\nHe further claimed that the projects had initially been suspended following a decision taken at the District Development Committee meeting held on January 29. However, the projects were later recommenced without the required fresh approval or review by the committee."
  },
