    "language": "en",
    "md5": "f2d871447b0658dd7c67adaedcf9c39e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1854,
    "chunk_text": "He further claimed that the projects had initially been suspended following a decision taken at the District Development Committee meeting held on January 29. However, the projects were later recommenced without the required fresh approval or review by the committee.\n\nHe also questioned whether the required environmental approvals had been properly obtained under the National Environmental Act. Citing provisions under Section 23B of the Act, he noted that large-scale development projects and power generation facilities exceeding the stipulated capacity must undergo a formal Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA) process and secure prior written approval.\n\n\u201cAny development exceeding one hectare or power projects above the prescribed capacity must obtain clearance following a proper environmental impact assessment,\u201d he said. \u201cCarrying out such projects without adhering to these legal requirements is a violation of the law.\u201d\n\nChamikara further alleged that the resumption of the projects had taken place without subjecting them to a renewed environmental review, despite the earlier suspension decision.\n\nHe warned that the continued destruction of forest areas within wildlife management zones would aggravate human-elephant conflict and undermine conservation efforts in the Hambantota District.\n\n\u201cThe elephant management reserve exists to protect wildlife and maintain ecological balance. Clearing these lands for commercial gain not only violates environmental law but also threatens biodiversity and local livelihoods,\u201d he stressed.\n\nChamikara called for a transparent investigation into the alleged financial transactions and the environmental damage caused within the reserve. He urged authorities to ensure strict enforcement of environmental regulations and to hold accountable those responsible for any illegal activities."
