    "chunk_size_bytes": 2017,
    "chunk_text": "Guardians of the silent kingdom: Sri Lanka\u2019s wild soul and urgency of protection\n\nIn the half-light before sunrise, when the land still belongs to shadows and silence, Sri Lanka\u2019s wilderness begins its ancient ritual of awakening. A solitary leopard melts into the golden grasses of Yala National Park. A herd of elephants moves like living monuments through the forest corridors.\n\nHigh above, an eagle rides invisible currents of air, surveying a world that has existed long before humanity learned to name it.\n\nThis is the silent kingdom. A realm without borders, without politics, without human ambition. Yet it is a kingdom increasingly under threat.\n\nAs Sri Lanka joins the global community in observing World Wildlife Day 2026, the occasion arrives not merely as a symbolic event, but as a solemn reminder of what is at stake. Declared by the United Nations, World Wildlife Day is a global call to protect the extraordinary diversity of life that sustains our planet\u2014and ourselves.\n\nSri Lanka, an island celebrated for its astonishing biodiversity, stands at a critical crossroads. Within its forests, wetlands, and oceans lives a concentration of species found nowhere else on Earth. From elusive amphibians hidden beneath rainforest leaves to mighty whales roaming the surrounding seas, the island\u2019s wildlife forms a living tapestry of evolutionary wonder.\n\nSpeaking to The Island, Hasini Sarathchandra, Head of Communications at the Department of Wildlife Conservation, emphasised the deeper meaning behind World Wildlife Day and the urgency of conservation.\n\n\u201cWildlife is not separate from us. It is part of the same ecological system that sustains human life,\u201d she said. \u201cWorld Wildlife Day reminds us that every species plays a role in maintaining balance. When we lose wildlife, we lose part of the natural system that supports our own survival.\u201d\n\nHer words echo with quiet conviction, shaped by years of engagement with Sri Lanka\u2019s natural heritage and the mounting pressures it faces."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-02-23-islandlk-fce15cc8",
