    "chunk_text": "\u201cConservation cannot be achieved by government institutions alone,\u201d she said. \u201cIt requires the support of communities, the involvement of young people, and a national commitment to protecting our natural heritage. Every citizen has a role to play.\u201d\n\nThis year\u2019s World Wildlife Day theme, \u201cMedicinal and Aromatic Plants: Conserving Health, Heritage and Livelihoods,\u201d highlights the profound connection between biodiversity and human wellbeing. Many of Sri Lanka\u2019s traditional medicinal systems rely on plants found in forests and natural ecosystems. These plants support healthcare, livelihoods, and cultural traditions that have endured for centuries.\n\nHowever, overharvesting, habitat destruction, and illegal trade threaten these vital resources.\n\n\u201cMedicinal plants are an important part of our heritage,\u201d Sarathchandra noted. \u201cProtecting them ensures not only ecological balance, but also the continuation of traditional knowledge and sustainable livelihoods.\u201d\n\nAcross Sri Lanka, a series of events and educational programmes are being organised to mark World Wildlife Day. Schools, conservation groups, and government agencies are working together to raise awareness and inspire a deeper appreciation for wildlife.\n\nIn classrooms, children learn about endangered species. In communities, conservationists share knowledge about coexistence. In protected areas, Wildlife officers continue their vigilant work.\n\nThe choice of Yala National Park as the focal point for national celebrations carries a symbolic significance. Yala is more than a tourist destination\u2014it is a living sanctuary where ecosystems function in their natural harmony.\n\nHere, predators and prey maintain the balance of life. Forests protect watersheds. Wetlands support birds and aquatic species. Each component contributes to the stability of the whole.\n\nStanding within such a landscape, one cannot escape the profound realisation that wildlife conservation is ultimately about preserving life\u2019s interconnected web."
  },
