    "md5": "afd7d1f0fb08c6d3dc74a1aa8cd7f065",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1984,
    "chunk_text": "Here, predators and prey maintain the balance of life. Forests protect watersheds. Wetlands support birds and aquatic species. Each component contributes to the stability of the whole.\n\nStanding within such a landscape, one cannot escape the profound realisation that wildlife conservation is ultimately about preserving life\u2019s interconnected web.\n\nEnvironment Minister Dr Dhammika Patabendi and Wildlife Conservation Department Director GeneralRanjan Marasinghe with Wildlife officers at the Girithale Wildlife Training Centre\n\nHuman\u2013wildlife conflict remains a serious issue, particularly in rural areas where elephants and humans compete for land and resources. Climate change introduces new uncertainties, altering ecosystems in ways that scientists are still striving to understand.\n\nIllegal wildlife trade, driven by global demand, continues to threaten vulnerable species.\n\nSarathchandra emphasised that awareness and education remain key tools in addressing these challenges.\n\n\u201cWhen people understand the value of wildlife, they are more likely to protect it,\u201d she said. \u201cEducation helps build a culture of conservation that can last for generations.\u201d\n\nHope that future generations will inherit forests filled with birdsong. Hope that elephants will continue their ancient migrations. Hope that leopards will still roam free beneath Sri Lanka\u2019s starlit skies.\n\nWorld Wildlife Day serves as a reminder that conservation is not a distant or abstract concept. It is a daily responsibility shared by all.\n\nEvery forest protected, every illegal activity prevented, every young person inspired to care about nature, contributes to a larger effort.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s wildlife is more than a collection of species. It is part of the nation\u2019s identity. It shapes landscapes, supports ecosystems, and enriches culture.\n\nIn the quiet hours of dawn, when the wilderness awakens once more, the silent kingdom continues its ancient rhythm. It asks for nothing, demands nothing."
