    "md5": "daae5ecd498cc4ec6156844c22f6cb62",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2017,
    "chunk_text": "Agriculture is not Sri Lanka\u2019s past, it is its strategic future\n\nSri Lanka is not a nation that lacks agricultural pedigree. We are a civilisation shaped by irrigation engineering, water management systems, and disciplined cultivation practices that predate modern economic theory.\n\nFrom the reservoirs of Anuradhapura to the fertile plains across the island, agriculture was once not merely an industry \u2014 it was statecraft.\n\nYet in contemporary economic planning, agriculture has too often been treated as peripheral rather than strategic. Policy inconsistency, regulatory volatility, and short-term fiscal thinking weakened what should have remained a national competitive advantage.\n\nFor decades, successive administrations approached agriculture through fragmented initiatives rather than structured long-term strategy. Import-heavy economic models frequently appeared easier than building resilient domestic production ecosystems.\n\nSuch an approach may provide temporary fiscal breathing space, but it weakens self-sufficiency and rural confidence. Agriculture cannot be managed politically in five-year cycles. It requires industrial planning measured in decades.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s fertile soil and diverse agro-climatic zones remain under-leveraged assets. The private sector has demonstrated what is possible when agriculture is treated as serious enterprise rather than subsistence activity.\n\nEntrepreneurs such as Dudley Sirisena, Chairman of Araliya Rice, and the leadership behind Ratna Group have shown that agriculture can be structured as scalable industry.\n\nThrough vertical integration, industrial-scale processing, logistics infrastructure, farmer network development, brand positioning, and disciplined execution, they built enterprises with national impact.\n\nTheir visible success \u2014 including ownership of Rolls-Royce vehicles, helicopters, and private jets \u2014 should not be viewed cynically.\n\nSri Lankans should take pride when local entrepreneurs achieve global standards of wealth creation."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-02-24-dailyftlk-27cde4b6",
    "num": "dailyftlk-27cde4b6",
    "date_str": "2026-02-24",
    "description": "Agriculture is not Sri Lanka\u2019s past, it is its strategic future",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Agriculture-is-not-Sri-Lanka-s-past-it-is-its-strategic-future/4-788717",
