    "md5": "02c1b97d23d4e07908acd8cabaa8ade0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1607,
    "chunk_text": "Ratmalana Airport: The Truth, The Whole Truth, And Nothing But The Truth\n\nOn 15 February 2026, the day of the India vs Pakistan T20 cricket match, 15 executive jets (or \u2018bizjets\u2019) landed at the Colombo-Bandaranaike International Airport (BIA), Katunayake.\n\nAlthough Ratmalana is closer to the Premadasa Stadium, Khettarama, Colombo, no aircraft opted to operate to the Colombo International Airport Ratmalana (CIAR).\n\nMore than Rs 100 million was expended two years ago by the Airport and Aviation \u2013 Sri Lanka Limited (AASL) to upgrade the Ratmalana Airport Road and lighting. Yet the work remains incomplete, and the airport precinct is subject to flooding due to deliberate destruction of the perfectly good water drainage system that was in existence.\n\nIt is no wonder, then, that operators avoid coming to Ratmalana. This historic airport (RML/VCCC), the first in Ceylon, has a single, relatively short runway in the vicinity of which pilots must obey Visual Flight Rules (VFR). That is to \u2018see and be seen\u2019 in the absence of Instrument Landing aids. Have priorities been mixed up and money sent down the drain?\n\nThe air traffic control tower at RIA lacks 360-degree visibility. The view on the south side of the airport was initially blocked by a restroom, and later by unplanned, tall buildings including the Kotelawala Defence Academy (KDU) and Hospital. Adding insult to injury, the authorities have further shortened the available landing distance because the air traffic controllers cannot see landing aircraft at the touchdown point, when approaching from the Galle Road (southern) end."
  },
