    "md5": "44f4a281797d9d43ee350e68be6bdfaf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1941,
    "chunk_text": "The air traffic control tower at RIA lacks 360-degree visibility. The view on the south side of the airport was initially blocked by a restroom, and later by unplanned, tall buildings including the Kotelawala Defence Academy (KDU) and Hospital. Adding insult to injury, the authorities have further shortened the available landing distance because the air traffic controllers cannot see landing aircraft at the touchdown point, when approaching from the Galle Road (southern) end.\n\nIn the bottom of the picture one can also see the solid concrete wall at the Galle Road end of the runway. This is another potential source of danger to aircraft that may (a) accidentally undershoot or overrun during an approach to land from the other (Attidiya) end, or (b) have to abort a takeoff in the same direction in case of a sudden emergency (loss of power, for example.) I suppose the authorities believe that even though it has been documented that there are on average six overruns worldwide every year such accidents will never happen at CIAR.\n\nA reason given for the presence and viability of the notorious wall is that the advertisements on its Galle Road face generate revenue for the Sri Lanka Air Force (SLAF) Welfare Society. In fact, to celebrate the 75th anniversary of the SLAF, a bicycle race has been sponsored mainly by the same advertiser who pays millions to the SLAF for providing hoarding space on the wall. That, dear readers, is the \u2018naked truth\u2019 of this sad and potentially disastrous affair.\n\nAll that the aircraft owners and operators are asking for is replacement of the concrete wall with a less hazardous frangible (breaking on impact), transparent fence. With proper signage and warnings for road users, an observant motor vehicle driver could brake and stop in time to prevent collision with an uncontrolled, over-running aircraft that can be seen from a distance, thus providing adequate warning of imminent danger."
  },
